CSS BACKGROUND-SIZE
==================

background-size controls
HOW the background image
is scaled inside an element.

It affects ONLY image size,
NOT position and NOT repetition.


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR
================
If background-size is NOT written:

→ Image keeps original size
→ Image repeats by default

Default value:
background-size: auto;



AUTO
====
background-size: auto

Meaning:
→ Image keeps its original width & height

What happens:
→ No scaling
→ Image may repeat if space exists

Important:
→ auto does NOT stop repetition

Example:
.box {
    background-size: auto;
}

To stop repeat:
background-repeat: no-repeat;



COVER (DETAILED)
================

Core idea:
→ Make the ELEMENT fully filled with the  background image
→ Layout is more important than image edges

How cover works:
→ Image scales UP or DOWN
→ Aspect ratio is always preserved
→ Scaling continues until
  NO empty space remains

What the browser prioritizes:
→ Entire element must be covered

What the browser allows:
→ Cropping of image edges

Result:
→ No empty space
→ Image may be cropped
→ Element looks completely filled

Why cropping happens:
→ Image and element shapes differ
→ Extra parts overflow and get hidden
→ Controlled using background-position

Real-life example:
→ Zooming a photo
→ Until it fills the screen
→ Edges go outside the frame

Important:
→ cover does NOT disable repetition
→ Repetition usually becomes invisible

Best practice:
.box {
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: center;
}

Use cover when:
→ Hero sections
→ Banners
→ Full-screen backgrounds



CONTAIN (DETAILED)
=================

Core idea:
→ Show the COMPLETE image
→ Image is more important than layout

How contain works:
→ Image scales UP or DOWN
→ Aspect ratio is always preserved
→ Scaling stops as soon as
  full image becomes visible

What the browser prioritizes:
→ Entire image must be visible

What the browser allows:
→ Empty space inside element

Result:
→ No cropping at all
→ Image may look smaller
→ Empty space may appear

Why empty space appears:
→ Image stops scaling early
→ Element still has extra area

Real-life example:
→ Shrinking a photo
→ To fit inside a frame
→ Frame may show gaps

Important:
→ contain does NOT stop repetition
→ Image WILL tile if space exists

Always combine with:
background-repeat: no-repeat;

Best practice:
.box {
    background-size: contain;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: center;
}

Use contain when:
→ Logos
→ Icons
→ Product images
→ Any image where edges matter



CUSTOM SIZE (PIXELS)
===================
Exact width and height.

.box {
    background-size: 200px 100px;
}

What happens:
→ Image is resized
→ Repeats if space exists



CUSTOM SIZE (PERCENTAGE)
=======================
Relative to element size.

.box {
    background-size: 50% 50%;
}

Meaning:
→ Width = 50% of element
→ Height = 50% of element



SINGLE VALUE
============
background-size: 200px;

Meaning:
→ Width is set
→ Height becomes auto
→ Aspect ratio preserved
→ Repeats if space exists



MULTIPLE BACKGROUND IMAGES
=========================
Each image can have its own size.

.layered {
    background-size: cover, contain;
}



IMPORTANT RULE (REMEMBER THIS)
==============================

background-size
→ controls SCALING

background-repeat
→ controls TILING

background-position
→ controls PLACEMENT

They work independently.



BEST PRACTICE PATTERN
=====================

.box {
    background-size: cover;   /* or contain */
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: center;
}



COMMON MISTAKES
===============
→ Expecting cover to show full image
→ Expecting contain to fill full area
→ Forgetting repeat behavior
→ Forgetting to set element height



ONE-LINE MEMORY TRICK
====================

→ cover   = protect the LAYOUT
→ contain = protect the IMAGE
